+# Agent Note: Host user input admission
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-18-host-user-input-admission.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Host integrations can repeat a human input from retained history. Direct enqueue bypasses prompt validation; marking that input as plugin context requires each transcript consumer to recover its human meaning.
+
+## Decision
+
+[Session Controller](../../../../packages/api/session-controller/README.md) shares admission between wire prompts and trusted Host user submissions. Host submissions carry durable attachment references and new request identities. They validate attachment availability without consuming upload receipts. The validation waterfall completes before the serial acceptance event persists consumer records and before enqueue.
+
+User authorship remains in the message source. Consumers retain reexecution provenance separately. Chat and trajectory read that source without producer allowlists or identity overrides. A user message retained outside a turn remains user-authored without inventing execution coordinates. This replaces the input presentation policy described alongside [history selection](2026-09-16-history-selection.md).
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Presentation overrides:** Producer allowlists and inspector-provided input identities repair individual views but leave other consumers with a different classification.
+
+**Direct enqueue:** Reusing Agent.followup alone skips model, attachment and admission checks. Reusing the original request identity also conflates a fresh execution with a transport retry.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Human-triggered repeated inputs run ordinary user-input processing. Automatic retries and plugin-authored context retain their existing identities. Durable facts are not rolled back after acceptance failures. Tests cover shared validation, late rejection, retained attachments, fresh identity, interruption and transcript ordering. Existing logs are not rewritten.
